:"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/almic","html_url":"https://github.com/almic","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/almic/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/almic/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/almic/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/almic/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/almic/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/almic/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/almic/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/almic/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/almic/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-02-02T18:05:18Z","updated_at":"2026-02-02T18:21:34Z","body":"Unfortunately, my specific use case involves a modification that exposes the `handles_intersect_ray` method for developers, which I was forced to return from when there is an active action to prevent switching to other gizmo handles. I would have to create a contrived example.\n\nIn my own project, I handle \"non-changes\" by just returning from `commit_handle` without creating an undo-redo action. This should have been the implemented solution for gizmos where final mouse position must be different to produce a console log and create an undo-able action. I haven't tried it, but in theory moving the camera while dragging a gizmo, moving the mouse enough to update, but then ensuring the mouse stops on the same pixel, will drop the change made. Not sure if it's possible to move the camera though, I assume right-click cancels it immediately. This might be possible if the move function is bound to something else.\n\nEDIT: if there exists a key binding to snap the camera to an orthographic perspective, and without canceling an edit, then it is absolutely possible to drop certain changes if the mouse hasn't \"moved\" but still has caused a change to the gizmo. Most likely a suitable test would be to position a CSG shape and select the gizmo, then flip to an orthographic perspective, shift the mouse a pixel and then back, then release.\n\nEDIT 2: Yes, in theory with the Numpad keys you can position a shape, click and hold a handle, switch to front view, move 1 pixel back and forth, and it drops the action. In my 3 attempts I wasn't precise enough to do it...\n\nEDIT 3: Okay so I already am using a modified version of Godot that returns `true` by default so of course I can't do it. Feel free to attempt this with a very low mouse sensitivity!","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/3836820404/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null}]